+### JSON Patch — `applyPatch(document, patch)`
+
+Applies a [JSON Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902) — an array of `add`, `remove`,
+`replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` operations, each naming its location with a JSON Pointer — and
+returns the result.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { name: 'config', tags: ['a'], retries: 1 };
+
+applyPatch(document, [
+  { op: 'test', path: '/retries', value: 1 },
+  { op: 'replace', path: '/retries', value: 3 },
+  { op: 'add', path: '/tags/-', value: 'b' },
+  { op: 'move', from: '/name', path: '/id' },
+]);
+// { id: 'config', tags: ['a', 'b'], retries: 3 }
+```
+
+The document handed in is never touched: the patch is applied to a copy of it, which is what comes
+back, and the two share nothing — changing one afterwards leaves the other alone. The values a patch
+carries are copied into the document too, so keeping a patch around and changing it later cannot
+change a document already patched with it.
+
+A patch is all-or-nothing, as RFC 6902, section 5 requires. The first operation that fails throws,
+and since nothing is ever written to the document handed in, it is left as it was:
+
+- `InvalidPatchError` — the patch is not a valid JSON Patch, whatever document it is applied to: it
+  is not an array of operations, an operation has no known `op` or lacks a member that `op` needs,
+  a `path` or `from` is not a JSON Pointer, a `value` is not a JSON value, a `remove` targets the
+  whole document, or a `move` would move a value into one of its own children. The whole patch is
+  checked for these before any of it is applied, so a mistake in the last operation is reported as
+  one.
+- `PatchOperationError` — the patch is valid, but an operation failed against this document: the
+  location it names does not exist, an `add` names an array index past the end of the array, or a
+  location's parent is a string, number, boolean or `null`.
+- `TestFailedError` — a `test` found a value different from the one it was given. It derives from
+  `PatchOperationError`, so a caller using a patch as a conditional update can catch a failed
+  precondition apart from every other failure.
+
+All three derive from `JsonPatchError`, so one `catch` covers any failure of a patch.
+
+The operations read and write locations exactly as `resolvePointer` resolves them: own members only,
+array indices that match RFC 6901's `array-index`, and no path created along the way — adding to
+`/a/b` fails unless `/a` is already there. `-` is the one token the operations give a meaning of
+their own: it is the position after the last element of an array, which is where `add`, `move` and
+`copy` append, and nowhere for the operations that need their target to exist.
+
+Values outside the JSON data model — `undefined`, a function, `NaN`, a class instance, a cycle — are
+not documents or patch values. The types rule them out; a document that holds one anyway raises a
+`TypeError`, naming the location where it was found, rather than being copied into a result that no
+longer serialises.
+
