+
+# applyPatch returns a copy: the document handed in is never touched
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` has to return the patched document without modifying the one it is
+given, and RFC 6902, section 5 adds that a patch is all-or-nothing: if any operation fails, "the
+entire patch document MUST NOT be applied". Two ways to get there:
+
+- **structural sharing** — build a new document that reuses the subtrees no operation touched, and
+  copy only the path from the root to each changed location;
+- **a copy up front** — copy the document once, apply the operations to the copy in place, and
+  return it.
+
+Structural sharing is cheaper on a large document with a small patch, but the result it returns
+shares objects and arrays with the input. A caller that then changes the result — the services
+import this toolkit to update configuration and stored records, and go on working with what they get
+back — would silently change the document it came from, in exactly the places the patch did not
+touch. That is a worse bug than a slow patch, and it is invisible until some unrelated code reads the
+original again.
+
+There is a second, quieter aliasing problem: the values a patch carries in its `value` members, and
+the value a `copy` operation duplicates. Inserted as they are, the document would hold the very
+object the patch holds, or the same object twice.
+
+## Decision
+
+`applyPatch` copies the document once, with `cloneJson`, and applies the operations to the copy. The
+result shares no object and no array with the document handed in; either can be changed afterwards
+without the other noticing. Atomicity comes for free: nothing is ever written to the caller's
+document, so a failure halfway through a patch leaves it as it was, and the only cost of a failed
+patch is the copy that is thrown away.
+
+Every value that enters the document is copied too:
+
+- a `value` member is copied out of the patch while the patch is being checked, so a caller that
+  keeps a patch and changes it later cannot change a document patched with it;
+- `copy` duplicates the value it reads, so the document never holds the same object twice.
+
+`cloneJson` is also where the JSON data model is checked, because it has to walk the whole value
+anyway. A value JavaScript admits and JSON does not — `undefined`, a function, a symbol, a bigint,
+`NaN`, an infinity, a class instance, an object with a prototype of its own, a cycle — raises a
+`TypeError` naming the location where it was found. Without that check a `Date` would be copied into
+a document that no longer serialises, and a cycle would make the copy run until the stack gave out.
+
+The `TypeError` for a document outside the data model is deliberately **not** a `JsonPatchError`. The
+types say a document is a `JsonValue`; handing in something else is a programming error on the
+caller's side, not a patch that failed, and the two call for different fixes. A `value` member of a
+patch is different: the patch is external input, so the same check there is reported as
+`InvalidPatchError`, with the `TypeError` as its `cause`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The contract is simple to state and to rely on: `applyPatch` is a pure function of its two
+  arguments, and nothing it returns is entangled with anything it was given.
+- Patching costs a full copy of the document, whatever the size of the patch. That is the price of
+  the contract above, and it is paid even by a patch that fails. If a caller ever needs to patch a
+  document large enough for this to matter, the answer is a separate, explicitly named entry point
+  that shares structure — not a change to this one.
+- `cloneJson` doubles as the data-model boundary. Any future building block that writes to a
+  document gets the same check by using it.
+- Member names are written with `Object.defineProperty`, not by assignment, so that `__proto__` and
+  the other names JavaScript treats specially become ordinary own members of the copy. See dl-006.
