+
+# JSON Patch errors: checked up front, in a typed JsonPatchError hierarchy
+
+## Context
+
+A patch can fail for reasons that are nothing like each other, and a caller does different things
+about each: a patch with a mistyped `op` is a malformed request to reject; a `remove` of a member
+that is not there may mean the document changed under the caller's feet; a failed `test` is usually
+the expected outcome of a conditional update, and the caller retries. RFC 6902 keeps the first apart
+from the rest — section 3 and section 4 describe the shape of a patch, section 5 the errors an
+operation raises against a document — and gives `test` the particular status of an operation whose
+job is to fail.
+
+dl-001 settled the same question for JSON Pointer, with `JsonPointerError` and its two subclasses.
+JSON Patch is built on pointers, so it also has to decide what a caller sees when the pointer
+underneath fails.
+
+## Decision
+
+Four classes, in `src/json-patch-errors.ts`:
+
+- `JsonPatchError extends Error` — the base, so one `catch` covers any failure of a patch;
+- `InvalidPatchError extends JsonPatchError` — the patch is not a valid JSON Patch;
+- `PatchOperationError extends JsonPatchError` — an operation failed against this document;
+- `TestFailedError extends PatchOperationError` — a `test` found a different value.
+
+Each sets `name` to its own class name, as the pointer errors do, so the kind survives serialisation
+to a log.
+
+**The line between the first two is whether the document was needed to decide.** A failure that can
+be seen in the patch alone is an `InvalidPatchError`: no array of operations, an unknown or missing
+`op`, a missing or ill-typed `path`, `from` or `value`, a pointer that is not a JSON Pointer, a
+`value` outside the JSON data model, a `remove` of the whole document, a `move` into one of the
+source's own children. A failure that only this document produces is a `PatchOperationError`. This
+is the same reasoning as dl-002, which made a bad array index a resolution error rather than a syntax
+error because only the document can decide it.
+
+**The whole patch is checked before any of it is applied.** `checkPatch` walks every operation,
+parses its pointers and copies its values; only then is the document copied and the operations
+applied. So a patch whose last operation has a typo fails as an `InvalidPatchError` naming that
+operation, instead of a `PatchOperationError` about an earlier operation that happened to run into
+the mess the first one left — and a malformed patch costs nothing, since the document is not even
+copied.
+
+**`TestFailedError` is a subclass, not a sibling.** A `test` guarding the operations after it is the
+idiom RFC 6902 offers for a conditional update; a caller that wants to retry on a failed
+precondition and alert on everything else catches it alone. Making it a `PatchOperationError` keeps
+it inside the broad `catch` of callers that do not care.
+
+**A pointer failure is reported as a patch failure, with the pointer error as its `cause`.** Callers
+of `applyPatch` should not have to catch `JsonPointerError` as well; the pointer module is an
+implementation detail of the operations. So an `InvalidPointerError` raised while checking a `path`
+becomes an `InvalidPatchError`, a `PointerResolutionError` raised while walking the document becomes
+a `PatchOperationError`, and the original is kept in `cause` for whoever wants the detail.
+
+**Messages name the operation and the location, never the data.** Every message starts with
+`operation <index> ("<op>")`, so a failure points at one line of the patch, and quotes the pointer of
+the location that failed. As in dl-001, no value from the document is interpolated — including in a
+failed `test`, which says that the location "is not equal to the operation's `value`" and shows
+neither side. A patch failure therefore cannot leak the content of a payload into a log.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers have three useful `catch`es: `InvalidPatchError` ("the patch is wrong, fix the caller"),
+  `TestFailedError` ("the precondition did not hold, retry"), `PatchOperationError` ("the document is
+  not what the patch assumed"), and `JsonPatchError` for all of them.
+- The four classes are public API. Moving a condition from one to another is a breaking change for
+  the services that import the toolkit.
+- Debugging a failed `test` needs the document and the patch at hand, since the message shows
+  neither value. That is the deliberate trade made in dl-001, kept here.
+- Checking the whole patch first means a patch is walked twice: once to check, once to apply. Both
+  are proportional to the patch, which is small next to the copy of the document.
