+
+# JSON Patch scope: strict RFC 6902, the ambiguous points settled
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 is short, and JSON Patch implementations in the wild differ on several of the points it
+leaves to the reader — some create the objects a path needs, some let `add` write past the end of an
+array, some accept a patch that is not quite one. The toolkit is imported by several of the team's
+services, so a patch has to mean the same thing everywhere, and the README states that behaviour must
+follow the published standard. Each point below is settled once, in favour of the RFC, and covered by
+a test. This continues dl-002, which did the same for RFC 6901.
+
+## Decision
+
+**No location is created along the way.** Section 4.1 requires the parent of an `add`'s target to
+exist: adding at `/a/b` fails unless `/a` is already an object or an array. The toolkit does not
+create intermediate objects, however convenient that would be — a patch that relies on it would mean
+something different here than in any service that uses a strict implementation, and RFC 6902,
+appendix A.12 gives exactly this case as an error.
+
+**`add` accepts one index past the end of an array, and no more.** Section 4.1: an index equal to the
+length appends, an index "greater than the number of elements" is an error. `-` means the position
+after the last element, and is the token to use for an append. The operations that need their target
+to exist — `remove`, `replace`, `test`, and the `from` of `move` and `copy` — reject `-` and any
+index from the length up, which is what dl-002 already decided for resolution.
+
+**`remove` cannot target the whole document.** The RFC does not say what the result of a patch that
+removed its own root would be, because there is none: `applyPatch` has to return a document. The
+pointer `""` as the `path` of a `remove` is therefore an `InvalidPatchError`, as is the `from` of a
+`move`, which removes its source. The root is a fine target for `add`, `replace`, `move` and `copy`
+— all of them replace the document — and a fine source for `copy` and `test`.
+
+**`move` into one of its own children is rejected; `copy` into one is not.** Section 4.4 forbids the
+first: a value cannot be moved inside itself. The comparison is on reference tokens, not on the
+characters of the pointer, so `/a` is a proper prefix of `/a/b` but not of `/ab`. Section 4.5 says
+nothing of the sort for `copy`, and nothing goes wrong: the value is copied before it is added, so
+copying `/a` to `/a/c` nests a copy of `/a` inside it.
+
+**`replace` is `remove` then `add`, and keeps the first one's requirement.** Section 4.3 says the
+target must exist; replacing a member that is not there, or an array index past the end, is a
+`PatchOperationError` and not a silent `add`.
+
+**Members an operation does not recognise are ignored**, as section 4 requires: `{"op": "remove",
+"path": "/a", "value": 1}` is a valid `remove`. A duplicate member — appendix A.13's two `op`s — is
+not something `applyPatch` can see: it takes a parsed patch, in which JSON's last-one-wins rule has
+already resolved the duplicate. Catching it would mean parsing the patch text, which is not this
+function's job. A test records that behaviour so nobody reads appendix A.13 as a gap.
+
+**Equality for `test` is section 4.6's, member by member.** Strings, numbers and the three literals
+are equal when they are the same value; arrays when they have the same length and equal elements in
+the same order; objects when they have the same set of member names, in any order, with equal values.
+Members are compared with `Object.hasOwn`, so an object's inherited names take no part in it, and
+JavaScript's coercions take none either: `1` and `"1"` are different, as appendix A.15 requires, and
+so are `null`, `false`, `0` and `""`.
+
+**The operations inherit the strict pointer rules.** Own members only, `array-index` enforced, the
+URI fragment form rejected: a location is read exactly as `resolvePointer` reads it. `-` is the one
+token the operations give a meaning of their own, which is the extension dl-002 anticipated.
+
+**Member names that mean something to JavaScript are ordinary member names.** `{"__proto__": 1}` is
+valid JSON, so `add`, `replace`, `copy` and `move` must be able to write a member called
+`__proto__` — and must not let it change the prototype of the object instead, which plain assignment
+would do, since `Object.prototype` has a setter for that name. Every write goes through
+`Object.defineProperty`, which makes the member own, enumerable and writable whatever its name. On
+the reading side, `Object.hasOwn` keeps `toString`, `constructor` and the rest of the prototype out
+of reach, so no patch can walk into JavaScript's machinery through a document.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A patch that applies here applies the same way in every service, and a patch that a lenient
+  implementation would stretch — creating a path, appending past the end, removing the root — fails
+  loudly instead of producing a document nobody asked for.
+- Callers migrating from a lenient implementation will see new errors, and have to spell out the
+  intermediate objects their patches used to get for free. That is the intent; the message names the
+  operation and the location, so the fix is local.
+- The toolkit cannot be used to check the shape of a patch *document* (duplicate members, numbers
+  that JSON cannot represent). That belongs to whatever parses the request body.
+- Prototype pollution through a patched document is closed off by construction, not by a blocklist of
+  names: there is no name the writes treat specially, and no inherited name the reads can reach.
