+
+# Module layout: the toolkit's modules share internals without publishing them
+
+## Context
+
+JSON Patch is the first building block built on another one. It needs more of JSON Pointer than
+`resolvePointer` gives: the reference tokens of a pointer (to reach the *parent* of a location, and
+to tell whether one location is a child of another), a walk along tokens that are already parsed, a
+way to write tokens back into a pointer for a message, and the `array-index` rule — which `add`
+applies with a bound one larger than resolution does.
+
+Three ways to get them: re-parse pointers in the patch module, which would mean two readings of
+RFC 6901 in one library; publish the helpers as API, which freezes them; or export them from their
+module for the toolkit's own use without re-exporting them from `src/index.ts`.
+
+The patch feature itself is also too big for one module: the operation format and its checks, the six
+operations, the copy of a document, and four error classes.
+
+## Decision
+
+`src/index.ts` stays the published surface, and nothing else is published. A module may export what
+the toolkit's other modules need, with a doc comment that says so — "shared with the other modules of
+the toolkit […] not part of the published API". `src/json-pointer.ts` exports `parsePointer`,
+`resolveTokens`, `formatPointer` and `parseArrayIndex` on that footing, and `resolvePointer` is now
+two lines on top of the first two.
+
+The modules, each with one job, in dependency order:
+
+- `src/json-value.ts` — the data model: the types, `isJsonObject`, and `describeJson`, which names a
+  value's kind for a message. It imports nothing, so every other module can use it.
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — RFC 6901: resolution, the pointer errors, and the shared pointer internals.
+- `src/json-document.ts` — what an operation needs of a document as a whole: `cloneJson`, the copy
+  that doubles as the data-model check (dl-004), and `setMember`, the write that keeps `__proto__`
+  an ordinary member (dl-006).
+- `src/json-patch-errors.ts` — the four error classes, in their own module so that the two modules
+  below both import them without a cycle.
+- `src/json-patch-operation.ts` — the operation types a caller writes a patch with, and `checkPatch`,
+  which turns a patch handed in at run time into operations the rest can trust.
+- `src/json-patch.ts` — RFC 6902 itself: `applyPatch` and the six operations.
+
+The dependencies form a chain, with no cycle: the patch modules use the document and pointer modules,
+which use the data model.
+
+The operation types (`PatchOperation` and the six, plus `JsonPatch`) *are* published, so a caller can
+write a patch in TypeScript and be told about a missing `value` before running anything. They
+describe the format RFC 6902 fixes, so publishing them costs no freedom. The checked form the modules
+pass around internally (`CheckedOperation`) is not published.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- One reading of RFC 6901 in the library. A change to the pointer rules reaches the operations
+  without being copied into them.
+- The published surface is what `src/index.ts` lists, and the one file to read to know it. The
+  exported-but-unpublished helpers can be changed in a single commit; the comment on each says as
+  much, and nothing outside the repository can be relying on them while the package has no `exports`
+  map (still a follow-up from task-001).
+- Six small modules instead of two large ones. Each one is read in a sitting, at the cost of more
+  files to navigate.
+- `erasableSyntaxOnly` and `verbatimModuleSyntax` (dl-003) hold: the types are imported with
+  `import type`, and no module needs syntax Node's type stripping cannot erase.
