+---
+id: task-002-implement-rfc-6902-json-patch-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement RFC 6902 JSON Patch applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Implement RFC 6902 JSON Patch applyPatch
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches: implement JSON Patch as specified
+in RFC 6902, with its six operations — `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` —
+exporting `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a patch (an array of
+operations) to a JSON document and returns the result as a new value, without modifying its input,
+and throws when the patch is invalid or one of its operations fails.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test-first, red before green: `test/json-patch.test.ts` was written and run against a missing
+`applyPatch`, then the implementation was added until all 130 cases passed. The 43 cases of
+task-001 were kept green throughout, as the characterization of the pointer module the operations are
+built on.
+
+### Code
+
+- `src/json-patch.ts` — `applyPatch`, the six operations, and the equality of RFC 6902, section 4.6.
+- `src/json-patch-operation.ts` — the operation types (`PatchOperation` and the six, `JsonPatch`) and
+  `checkPatch`, which checks a patch handed in at run time and returns its operations with their
+  pointers parsed and their values copied out of the patch.
+- `src/json-patch-errors.ts` — `JsonPatchError`, `InvalidPatchError`, `PatchOperationError` and
+  `TestFailedError`.
+- `src/json-document.ts` — `cloneJson`, the copy of a document that doubles as the JSON data-model
+  check, and `setMember`, the write that keeps `__proto__` an ordinary member name.
+- `src/json-value.ts` — gained `isJsonObject` and `describeJson`, which the modules above and
+  `json-pointer.ts` now share.
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — unchanged in behaviour; `parsePointer`, `resolveTokens`, `formatPointer`
+  and `parseArrayIndex` are now exported for the toolkit's own use, and `resolvePointer` is written on
+  top of them.
+- `src/index.ts` — publishes `applyPatch`, the four error classes and the operation types, next to
+  what task-001 published.
+
+`applyPatch` checks the whole patch first, then copies the document and applies each operation to the
+copy in order: a location is read exactly as `resolvePointer` reads it, written through the parent
+that holds it, and `-` means the position after the last element of an array.
+
+### Tests
+
+`test/json-patch.test.ts`, 130 cases: the examples of RFC 6902, appendix A, including the three that
+must fail; each operation's happy path and its error paths; the root as a target and as a source; the
+equality rules of section 4.6, member by member, as a table of equal and different pairs; the
+document and the patch handed in being left alone and sharing nothing with the result; atomicity when
+an operation halfway through a patch fails; every way a patch can be invalid; documents outside the
+JSON data model; and the member names that reach JavaScript's machinery — `__proto__` written and
+read as an ordinary member, inherited names resolving nowhere.
+
+### Documentation
+
+`README.md` gained a "JSON Patch" building block: the example, the immutability contract, the three
+error conditions, the strict reading of the locations, and what happens to a value outside the JSON
+data model.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- dl-004 — `applyPatch` copies the document and the patch's values, rather than sharing structure.
+- dl-005 — the error hierarchy, the line between an invalid patch and a failed operation, checking
+  the whole patch up front, and messages that name the operation but never the data.
+- dl-006 — the strict reading of RFC 6902: no location created along the way, `add`'s bound of one
+  past the end, no `remove` of the root, no `move` into a child, unrecognised members ignored,
+  section 4.6 equality, and `__proto__` as an ordinary member name.
+- dl-007 — the module layout, and modules sharing internals without publishing them.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 173 tests, 173 pass, 0 fail (130 for JSON Patch, 43 for JSON Pointer).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`, with relative imports rewritten to `.js`.
+- `tsc --noUnusedLocals --noUnusedParameters` over sources and tests reports nothing.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- Still no linter, and `package.json` still declares no `main` or `exports`: both follow-ups of
+  task-001 stand, and the second one now matters more, since the published surface has grown.
+- `applyPatch` copies the whole document on every call. Fine for configuration files and API
+  payloads; if a caller ever patches a document large enough for the copy to show, dl-004 says what
+  to do about it.
